---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import OrcaNav from "../../../components/OrcaNav.astro";
import OrcaFooter from "../../../components/OrcaFooter.astro";
import { worksToday, inReview, notYet, phases, ORCA_REPO, DISTA_SECTION } from "../../../data/orca";

const description =
  "orca orchestrates data pipelines written in Mojo: DAGs declared in Mojo and checked by the compiler, steps that run on dista, datasets in Iceberg tables and state in Postgres. Early, open source, phase 1 of five.";
---

<Base title="orca: orchestration for Mojo data pipelines" description={description} bare chrome={false}>
  <OrcaNav />

  <section class="section section--flush">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">orca</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Data pipelines, declared in Mojo</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        orca is the layer that decides when work runs, in what order, and what happens when
        a step fails. A pipeline is a DAG of steps written in Mojo, and the compiler checks
        that each step takes what the step before it produces. The heavy work inside a step
        runs on <a href={DISTA_SECTION}>dista</a>, the data it reads and writes lives in
        Iceberg tables, and orca's own record of runs lives in Postgres. Schedules and a web
        UI come later; today a pipeline is a binary you run.
      </p>

      <div class="two-col orca-intro">
        <div class="prose">
          <pre><code>{`from dista import Seq
from orca import Ctx, Gather, Mapped, Month, Pipeline, Step, orca_main

def size(mut ctx: Ctx, m: Month) raises -> Int:
    return m.month * 100

def total(mut ctx: Ctx, sizes: Seq[Int]) raises -> Int:
    var t = 0
    for i in range(len(sizes)):
        t += sizes[i]
    return t

comptime Size = Step["size", Month, Int, size, Mapped]
comptime Total = Step["total", Seq[Int], Int, total, Gather, after=Size]
comptime Sizes = Pipeline["sizes", Size, Total]

def main() raises:
    orca_main[Sizes]()`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            <code>Size</code> runs once per month of the run; <code>Total</code> runs once and
            gets all of their outputs as a <code>Seq</code>. Declaring <code>Total</code> with
            an input that isn't a <code>Seq[Int]</code> doesn't compile. The binary gets a
            command line: <code>./sizes run --partitions 2024-01..2024-03</code> runs it, and
            with <code>ORCA_DATABASE_URL</code> set, every step and attempt is recorded so a
            run that was killed can be resumed where it stopped.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="callout">
          <h3 class="callout__title">Who it's for</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>People building data pipelines in Mojo</strong> on a data lake, who want
            them run in order, observed and rerunnable without a Python orchestrator in front
            of compiled code.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Dataset builds</strong>: ingest raw data by partition, clean and join it
            into Iceberg tables, publish a new version. The NYC taxi load that dista's
            benchmarks read is the first one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Multi-stage processing</strong>, where each stage is a distributed job
            reading the previous stage's table, such as detection, then tracking, then
            lifting to 3D over driving logs.
          </p>
          <p class="callout__fine">
            <a href="/s/orca/docs">Read the docs</a> ·
            <a href={ORCA_REPO} rel="noopener">source on GitHub</a>
          </p>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">An orchestrator that knows the data lake</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Dagster and Airflow are Python. A Mojo step runs behind a Python definition, and the
        compiler checks neither against the other. They track runs of code, not which
        Iceberg snapshot a step read or wrote. orca is built from the same tins as the rest
        of magmalake, and keeps track of the data as well as the code.
      </p>
      <div class="orca-points">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3 class="panel__title">Edges are type checks</h3>
          <p>
            A step's input type must match what its upstream produces, checked with
            <code>comptime assert</code>s when the pipeline is built. There is no YAML and no
            templating.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3 class="panel__title">Reruns read the same data</h3>
          <p>
            A step's reads are pinned to the snapshot its first attempt saw, so a retry or a
            resume sees the same rows whatever was committed in between.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3 class="panel__title">Commits are recognised</h3>
          <p>
            Every snapshot a step commits carries the run, step, partition and attempt in its
            summary. A retry after a crash finds its own commit instead of loading the month
            again.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3 class="panel__title">Small</h3>
          <p>
            One binary per pipeline, which is also its dista driver and workers, and one
            Postgres database. The scheduler, when it arrives, is one more process.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">Status</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Phase 1 of {phases.length}: DAGs and runs</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        orca is in its first phase, DAGs and runs. Steps, pipelines, state in Postgres and
        pinned Iceberg reads are merged; dista inside steps, logs, and fuzzing and chaos
        testing are in review. The phase ends when the 2023–2024 NYC taxi load runs as a
        pipeline and a run killed halfway resumes at the month that failed.
        The <a href="/s/orca/roadmap">roadmap</a> has every phase and its gate.
      </p>
      <div class="status-grid">
        <div class="status-col status-col--yes">
          <h3 class="status-col__head">Works today</h3>
          <ul>{worksToday.map((w) => <li>{w}</li>)}</ul>
        </div>
        <div class="status-col status-col--no">
          <h3 class="status-col__head">Not yet</h3>
          <ul>
            {inReview.map((r) => <li>In review: {r}</li>)}
            {notYet.map((n) => <li>{n}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="panel__fine orca-fine">
        No benchmark numbers yet: the first measured run is the taxi pipeline at the end of
        phase 1.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <OrcaFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  .orca-intro { margin-top: 2rem; }
  .orca-intro pre { margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }
  .orca-points {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(14rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  .orca-fine { margin-top: 1rem; max-width: var(--measure); }
</style>
